<rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom" xmlns:media="http://search.yahoo.com/mrss/" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"><channel><language>en</language><title>News from Europe - Latest world headlines and daily news - France 24</title><description>FRANCE 24 explores events across Europe, including in the 27 member states of the European Union and the United Kingdom. Follow our on-the-ground reports, interviews and analyses of the events affecting social and political life on the Continent and Europe’s role in the world.</description><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/</link><lastBuildDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 14:07:03 GMT</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/rss" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><category>Europe</category><title>Live: Russian strikes on Ukraine kill at least 15 in Zaporizhzhia, cause power outages in Kyiv</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261010-live-russian-strikes-on-ukraine-zaporizhzhia-kill-least-seven-including-children</link><description>
Russian strikes on the southeastern Ukrainian city of Zaporizhzhia killed at least 15 people, including three children, regional authorities said on Saturday. The attack also caused "large-scale power outages" in the capital Kyiv and the surrounding region. Follow our liveblog for the latest developments. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9e19554c-c4a8-11f1-afba-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T064056Z-387380484-RC2GZNAV9HM8-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-ATTACK-ZAPORIZHZHIA.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9e19554c-c4a8-11f1-afba-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T064056Z-387380484-RC2GZNAV9HM8-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-ATTACK-ZAPORIZHZHIA.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">6cbaa19a-c485-11f1-9885-e513ae9fb83b</guid><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 08:41:39 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/9e19554c-c4a8-11f1-afba-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T064056Z-387380484-RC2GZNAV9HM8-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-ATTACK-ZAPORIZHZHIA.jpg">© Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>'All human emotions': French astronaut Sophie Adenot returns to Earth</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261010-all-human-emotion-french-astronaut-sophie-adenot-first-words-since-returning-earth</link><description>
French astronaut Sophie Adenot said she experienced “the full range of human emotions, from the most joyful to the most difficult” during her eight months in space, speaking on Saturday in Cologne, Germany, in her first public appearance since returning to Earth.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4dc35d4a-c471-11f1-89b7-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-10T055758Z-1398580967-RC250OAPT20V-RTRMADP-3-GERMANY-ESA-ASTRONAUT.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4dc35d4a-c471-11f1-89b7-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-10T055758Z-1398580967-RC250OAPT20V-RTRMADP-3-GERMANY-ESA-ASTRONAUT.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b183a050-c481-11f1-8de0-8f0559b72f8a</guid><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 08:09:18 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4dc35d4a-c471-11f1-89b7-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-10T055758Z-1398580967-RC250OAPT20V-RTRMADP-3-GERMANY-ESA-ASTRONAUT.jpg">© Thilo Schmuelgen, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>‘So safe you could drink it’: Scientists and pesticide victims fear an EU rollback</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261010-so-safe-you-could-drink-it-scientists-and-pesticide-victims-fear-an-eu-rollback</link><description>
For decades, French farmers sprayed pesticides they were told were harmless. Many have since fallen ill, grappling with cancers and other diseases caused by exposure. As the EU prepares to vote on a package that would end regular safety reviews of most pesticides, victims, doctors and scientists warn it could keep harmful products on the market for longer.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cdd5d364-c3e0-11f1-8680-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25132558305552.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cdd5d364-c3e0-11f1-8680-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25132558305552.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">2418163a-c3df-11f1-882c-f7781fb761f1</guid><pubDate>Sat, 10 Oct 2026 07:10:25 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/cdd5d364-c3e0-11f1-8680-3774aeac812a/w:1024/p:16x9/AP25132558305552.jpg">© Charlie Neibergall, AP</source><dc:creator>Lara BULLENS</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Italy's biggest-ever wine heist</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261009-italy-s-biggest-ever-wine-heist</link><description>
A multi million euro wine heist in Italy. As thieves in Tuscany steal 30 thousand bottles of the one of the most iconic labels in the country from a warehouse of the the prized Marchesi Antinori winery as it prepared for a major export. The company said in the past few hours, they believe two vans and up to six thieves were involved. Gavin Lee speaks to France 24's correspondent in Rome, Seema Gupta. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/773c6e38-c411-11f1-9d07-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/NW257983-O-01-20261009-01.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/773c6e38-c411-11f1-9d07-29a2e29ae696/w:1024/p:16x9/NW257983-O-01-20261009-01.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d15385ce-c410-11f1-bf61-d38a4bd206bd</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:08:41 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Trump strikes diesel deal with Putin, reversing years of pressure on Russia over Ukraine war</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261009-trump-strikes-diesel-deal-with-putin-reversing-years-of-pressure-on-russia-over-ukraine-war</link><description>
US President Donald Trump said Friday that Russia had agreed to immediately supply more than 300,000 tonnes of diesel to US and global markets in an effort to cut fuel prices before November's midterm elections. The move marks a stunning change of policy after the US and its allies hit Russia with waves of sanctions over its invasion of Ukraine. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/12b624b2-c417-11f1-bda1-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C4EQ663-1.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/12b624b2-c417-11f1-bda1-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C4EQ663-1.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">72a8fbd2-c418-11f1-813d-35a9deac8e44</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 20:00:24 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/12b624b2-c417-11f1-bda1-e362370cce2e/w:1024/p:16x9/000-C4EQ663-1.jpg">© Jim Watson, Kristina Solovyova, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Talking Europe</category><title>EU's Covid recovery fund was 'absolutely a success': European Court of Auditors' Pierre Moscovici</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/talking-europe/20261009-eu-s-covid-recovery-fund-was-absolutely-a-success-european-court-of-auditors-pierre-moscovici</link><description>
2020 was an annus horribilis. Covid stalked the land. Lockdowns, curfews and social distancing were the order of the day. But there was a silver lining: European countries showed themselves to be capable of great solidarity, jointly procuring vaccines and then agreeing on a mammoth post-Covid recovery fund, the Recovery and Resilience Facility (RRF). That fund has disbursed €450 billion in grants and loans to EU member states since 2021. As the RRF enters its crucial final phase, we take stock of the situation with Pierre Moscovici, the French member of the European Court of Auditors (ECA).
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b41572ae-c3cc-11f1-ba89-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/PHOTO-MOSCOVICI.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/b41572ae-c3cc-11f1-ba89-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/PHOTO-MOSCOVICI.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e2105508-c3cb-11f1-8c99-09a6c5db97b1</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:51:06 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Armen GEORGIAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Israel a rare dissenting voice as praise pours in for Nobel Peace Prize winner Navi Pillay</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261009-israel-a-rare-dissenting-voice-as-praise-pours-in-for-nobel-peace-prize-winner-navi-pillay</link><description>
Advocates of human rights and international justice cheered, even as Israeli officials fumed, after South African jurist and former UN human rights chief Navi Pillay won the Nobel Peace Prize on Friday. The 85-year-old drew wide praise for decades of work, including investigating crimes in Rwanda, Israel and the occupied Palestinian territories. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4cee3a6e-c3c9-11f1-bbfc-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T091255Z-1569360422-RC2LZNAVP694-RTRMADP-3-NOBEL-PRIZE-PEACE.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4cee3a6e-c3c9-11f1-bbfc-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T091255Z-1569360422-RC2LZNAVP694-RTRMADP-3-NOBEL-PRIZE-PEACE.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">3aefb3ca-c3f0-11f1-8c9b-8399f5a7519d</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:20:05 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/4cee3a6e-c3c9-11f1-bbfc-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-09T091255Z-1569360422-RC2LZNAVP694-RTRMADP-3-NOBEL-PRIZE-PEACE.jpg">© Valentin Flauraud, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>International Criminal Court faces 'test of resilience' as US imposes sweeping sanctions</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261009-international-criminal-court-faces-test-of-resilience-as-us-imposes-sweeping-sanctions</link><description>
The Trump administration has imposed sweeping new sanctions on the International Criminal Court as part of its campaign to dismantle what it describes as a "rogue" tribunal, threatening to cripple its operations. Following the move, ⁠the European Union said it was "ready to take any necessary measures to enable the court to carry out its work".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d931fb3e-c3f4-11f1-88f8-77317c09f325/w:1024/p:16x9/9539cbb9cbf53a71e89a1f4342b3158fa146caca.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d931fb3e-c3f4-11f1-88f8-77317c09f325/w:1024/p:16x9/9539cbb9cbf53a71e89a1f4342b3158fa146caca.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">d9fba97a-c3f4-11f1-8953-65aa7013460f</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:19:41 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/d931fb3e-c3f4-11f1-88f8-77317c09f325/w:1024/p:16x9/9539cbb9cbf53a71e89a1f4342b3158fa146caca.jpg">© Remko de Waal, ANP via AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Talking Europe</category><title>Falling test scores in schools startle EU countries: Why are standards going down?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/talking-europe/20261009-falling-test-scores-in-schools-startle-eu-countries-why-are-standards-going-down</link><description>
It has been dubbed "the PISA shock". Countries that had been used to their school pupils performing well in test scores are seeing a plunge in standards. The PISA test is an international assessment led by the Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD), which measures how well 15‑year‑olds in over 80 countries apply reading, mathematics and science skills to real‑world problems. In the latest rankings, France's results were the worst it has ever had, and Germany's school pupils got their worst PISA report card too.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/602dd6fc-c15d-11f1-9e8b-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/Debate-Pisa2.png"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/602dd6fc-c15d-11f1-9e8b-0f96b136895a/w:1024/p:16x9/Debate-Pisa2.png" type="image/png" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">e07f6244-c15d-11f1-915e-c1ae4f7ce44b</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 15:03:42 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>Armen GEORGIAN</dc:creator></item><item><category>Scoop</category><title>Lawsuits blame Fox News for deaths of journalists in Ukraine</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/scoop/20261009-lawsuits-blame-fox-news-for-deaths-of-journalists-in-ukraine</link><description>
This week, FRANCE 24's media show Scoop revisits a fatal assignment for a Fox News team in Ukraine. On March 14, 2022, a Russian strike hit the crew reporting near the capital Kyiv. Reporter Benjamin Hall was seriously injured and two other journalists were killed. The journalists' families are suing Fox, saying the network put the story ahead of its people. Our guest is NPR's David Folkenflik.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/df088b96-c3df-11f1-9db5-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-16543625836ac8e2dae116b5-18744395.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/df088b96-c3df-11f1-9db5-53993c5f1f18/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-16543625836ac8e2dae116b5-18744395.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">21d41366-c3de-11f1-8f1a-ff81f5596910</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 14:23:35 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>William HILDERBRANDT</dc:creator></item><item><category>Focus</category><title>Residents of Ukraine's Kramatorsk forced to flee as drone strikes intensify</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261009-residents-of-ukraine-s-kramatorsk-forced-to-flee-as-drone-strikes-intensify</link><description>
The city of Kramatorsk, in eastern Ukraine, is increasingly coming under Russian attack. For those who have stayed behind, the threat from drones and shelling has become a daily reality. Many have resisted leaving their homes, despite the growing danger – but now, more and more residents are being forced to flee. Our colleagues at France 2 followed an evacuation from the city and witnessed first-hand the dangers that residents face.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a254ec88-c3d7-11f1-a4ce-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-14410476136ac8d50910b737-10104573.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/a254ec88-c3d7-11f1-a4ce-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/capture-14410476136ac8d50910b737-10104573.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">b0984d42-c32f-11f1-937c-813a6b8358d6</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 11:51:07 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE 2</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>South Africa's Navi Pillay wins 2026 Nobel Peace Prize</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261009-south-africa-s-navanethem-navi-pillay-wins-2026-nobel-peace-prize</link><description>
Former UN human rights chief and International Court of Justice judge Navanethem "Navi" Pillay has won the 2026 Nobel Peace Prize for her contributions to international law, the Nobel jury announced on Friday. The Nobel committee denied it was taking a stance on whether Israel is committing genocide in Gaza by awarding Pillay, who led a UN inquiry on the subject, after Israel said honouring her legitimised "anti-Israel hatred".
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/879b9b0a-c3c1-11f1-8bec-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/000-337M979.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/879b9b0a-c3c1-11f1-8bec-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/000-337M979.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">9b46e72a-c3b9-11f1-a8b1-759d170e0a5b</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 09:03:34 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/879b9b0a-c3c1-11f1-8bec-ef6152f87623/w:1024/p:16x9/000-337M979.jpg">© Wikus de Wet, AFP</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>French student protests: Police impunity ‘below' the standards of a 'well-functioning democracy’</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261009-french-student-protests-police-impunity-below-the-standards-of-a-well-functioning-democracy-1</link><description>
Erin Ogunkeye welcomes Dr. Antoine Marie, postdoctoral researcher, fellow and lecturer at Sciences Po / CEVIPOF, to explore the political psychology behind France’s student protests. Why do people witness the same events yet reach radically different conclusions about violence? Dr Marie examines how media echo chambers, partisan thinking and the politicization of empathy deepen polarization, making it easier to overlook the suffering of those perceived as political opponents. He also examines France’s increasingly offensive police doctrine, questions surrounding accountability, and the institutional incentives that may actually hinder independent investigations into police misconduct.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/977a2c78-c347-11f1-a553-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261008-151311-152324-CS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/977a2c78-c347-11f1-a553-c55799ef8309/w:1024/p:16x9/EN-20261008-151311-152324-CS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">a8feee3e-c347-11f1-bc8e-5ff5dfc17852</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 06:55:58 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>FRANCE24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Tens of thousands march in Madrid to mourn pensioner whose eviction sparked housing protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261009-tens-of-thousands-march-in-madrid-to-mourn-pensioner-whose-eviction-sparked-housing-protests</link><description>
Tens of thousands of protesters joined a march in Madrid Thursday to mark the death of 87-year-old retiree Maricarmen Abascal, whose eviction from her apartment in the Spanish capital in September sparked a wave of housing protests that prompted the government to call early elections.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/56551082-c3ab-11f1-902b-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T183913Z-944686721-RC26ZNABD0G8-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-HOUSING-MARICARMEN-PROTEST.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/56551082-c3ab-11f1-902b-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T183913Z-944686721-RC26ZNABD0G8-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-HOUSING-MARICARMEN-PROTEST.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">976b23ec-c3a9-11f1-a540-8d0d67d73d24</guid><pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 06:45:35 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/56551082-c3ab-11f1-902b-f50f6d982dc3/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T183913Z-944686721-RC26ZNABD0G8-RTRMADP-3-SPAIN-HOUSING-MARICARMEN-PROTEST.jpg">© Violeta Santos Moura, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>Europe</category><title>Trump envoys, European officials to discuss ‘new ideas’ for Ukraine peace in Miami</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261008-trump-envoys-ukraine-peace-talks-miami</link><description>
US President Donald Trump’s envoys will meet Ukrainian negotiators and European officials in Miami on Friday to discuss a new proposal to end Russia’s war in Ukraine. Steve Witkoff and Jared Kushner will present ideas developed during recent talks with both sides, one source said Thursday.
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/522e42a4-aa19-11f1-b141-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-06T161535Z-722947549-RC2SDNAERMLR-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-USA-ENVOYS.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/522e42a4-aa19-11f1-b141-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-06T161535Z-722947549-RC2SDNAERMLR-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-USA-ENVOYS.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">ad372da8-c35c-11f1-a196-5577fbbf20aa</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 21:19:29 GMT</pubDate><source url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/522e42a4-aa19-11f1-b141-005056a90284/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-09-06T161535Z-722947549-RC2SDNAERMLR-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-USA-ENVOYS.jpg">© Valentyn Ogirenko, Reuters</source><dc:creator>FRANCE 24</dc:creator></item><item><category>The Debate</category><title>Black Sea and black ops: How exposed is Europe to Ukraine war spillover?</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261008-black-sea-and-black-ops-how-exposed-is-europe-to-ukraine-war-spillover</link><description>
The story is as underreported as it is consequential for the entire planet. Three Turkish-owned cargo ships out of Ukraine have been hit in EU waters by suspected Russian drones. One, the Togo-flagged Alfa Watan, sunk on Tuesday off Bulgaria. Ten Egyptian and Syrian crew members are missing. For its part, Ukraine has been hitting Russian tankers in the Black Sea, with no sign of a return to a UN-brokered deal to allow oil, grain and fertiliser ships to pass. 
</description><media:thumbnail url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c4d9528e-c342-11f1-b42c-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T155502Z-91069183-RC2XYNAVY1S2-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-ATTACK-KYIV-REGION.jpg"/><enclosure url="https://s.france24.com/media/display/c4d9528e-c342-11f1-b42c-27c2853ad5a5/w:1024/p:16x9/2026-10-08T155502Z-91069183-RC2XYNAVY1S2-RTRMADP-3-UKRAINE-CRISIS-ATTACK-KYIV-REGION.jpg" type="image/jpeg" length="0"/><guid isPermaLink="false">fb14136c-c341-11f1-913a-1f7dde354c7d</guid><pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 20:42:28 GMT</pubDate><dc:creator>François PICARD</dc:creator></item><item><category></category><title>Spain mourns Maricarmen Abascal, whose eviction sparked housing protests</title><link>https://www.france24.com/en/spain-mourns-maricarmen-abascal-whose-eviction-sparked-housing-protests</link><description>
Protests and anger have grown across Spain after the death of an 87-year-old woman in the wake of her eviction from her home. Maricarmen Abascal was thrown out of her home of seven decades in what became the "cause celebre" of Spain's housing crisis. FRANCE 24's Sarah Morris reports from Madrid.
